Section 3— Maternal Health
‘soseeeeennsnsosensnnnonesesnesesssssensssssssssssessnseseenestsaeusnnesansasssssnsssnssseasetansnsuscussseusassascscnessssnnsannanenninsionnicienstuciaemes
MCQ 3.312
A 20-year-old woman, (gravida 1, para 0), who is at term, has been in labour for
almost eight hours. The membranes ruptured two hours prior to the commencement
of the contractions. The fetal heart rate is regular at 144/min. The contractions are of
good quality, occurring approximately every 2-3 minutes, and lasting 45 seconds.
The cervix has been fully dilated for one hour, and she has been pushing for most of
this time. The vertex is judged to be in the occipito-anterior (OA) position and has
descended from 0 station to a level 2cm below the ischial spines in the last hour.
Which one of the following best describes the situation at this point in time?
A. Obstructed labour.
B. Maternal distress.
C. Delay in the second stage.
D. Cephalopelvic disproportion.
E. Normal progress.
sseeeeeeesssssenensesensnesnnsessessnemsmsnunssassnseasesenenssnsessensnassssssnasessssusassasuasssususssseasseusssstenssnassensessuuasensesstssssssnssensnnstsaauuseeusessusasseneneeeeseens
MCQ 3.313
A 27-year-old nulliparous woman is admitted to hospital at 37 weeks of gestation
having lost about 200mL of blood per vaginum after having had sexual intercourse.
The bleeding has now ceased. On clinical examination the pulse rate is 64/min, BP
120/80mmHg and temperature 36.8°C. The uterus is enlarged to a level 37cm above
the pubic symphysis, is lax and non-tender, the presentation is cephalic with the head
still freely mobile above the pelvic brim. The fetal heart rate assessed by auscultation
is 155/min. Which one of the following is the most likely cause for the bleeding?','{"A":"Vasa praevia.","B":"Placental abruption.","C":"Placenta praevia.","D":"Acervical polyp.","E":"Aheavy show.\nMCQ 3.314"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[360],"source_qid":"3.312","duplicate_group_id":"exact-35e11f74ca2d","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"208bd62d9e423514b878","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":360,"source_qid":"3.312"}],"parser_version":"2.8-residual-resolver-selective-ocr-visual-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v28_selective_ocr_multi_group","text_method":"v28_selective_ocr","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,'text',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"208bd62d9e423514b878","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":360,"supporting_pages":[360],"source_qid":"3.312","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"Australian Medical Council /\nSection 3— Maternal Health\n‘soseeeeennsnsosensnnnonesesnesesssssensssssssssssessnseseenestsaeusnnesansasssssnsssnssseasetansnsuscussseusassascscnessssnnsannanenninsionnicienstuciaemes\nMCQ 3.312\nA 20-year-old woman, (gravida 1, para 0), who is at term, has been in labour for\nalmost eight hours. The membranes ruptured two hours prior to the commencement\nof the contractions. The fetal heart rate is regular at 144/min. The contractions are of\ngood quality, occurring approximately every 2-3 minutes, and lasting 45 seconds.\nThe cervix has been fully dilated for one hour, and she has been pushing for most of\nthis time. The vertex is judged to be in the occipit