Section 3-— Mental Health','{"A":"explains the mechanisms of some drug interactions with antidepressants","B":"increases the lipid solubility of the benzodiazepines","C":"directly enhances the toxicity of lithium carbonate","D":"is responsible for the central nervous system ‘rush effect’ of the psychostimulants","E":"is inhibited by depot antipsychotics.\n\nA 36-year-old man self-presents to the Emergency Department in a highly agitated\nand distressed state. He is a boilermaker at a local engineering workshop and has just\nwitnessed a fellow worker being struck in the eyes by molten metal fragments during\nan explosion at the factory that afternoon. The fellow worker has been rushed to the\nCity Eye Hospital. The man is saying that he is also blind and cannot see, but he was\nnot injured in the explosion and you can find no abnormalities on thorough physical\n\na examination. He also tells you that he feels responsible because he had been working\nf nearby with an oxyacetylene flame but had left it unattended whilst he went to the\nyy lavatory and this may have contributed to the explosion. Which one of the following\ng is the most appropriate immediate psychological intervention for his anguish?\n\nA. Working through.\nn B. Reality testing.\n\nC. Debriefing.\nd D. Comforting.\n\nE. Problem solving.\n1,\n\nMCQ 3.357\n\nAt the beginning of your morning shift in the Emergency Department you are asked\nby one of the nurses to ‘tidy up’ some paperwork left over from the night before. A\n6 patient who has now been discharged after treatment for a fractured radius was\ngiven pethidine, a narcotic analgesic which is strictly regulated by law. The nurse\ntells you that the night resident doctor ‘forgot’ to write it up because it was a very\nbusy night and he had been distracted by a cardiac arrest and then a motor vehicle\naccident. The nurse insists that it was given and is pressuring you to countersign the\ndrug register and retrospectively complete the patient''s treatment chart. Which one\nof the following is the most appropriate response to this request?\n\nRing up the night resident.\n\nContact your Medical Defence Association.\n\n. Refer the matter to the senior Emergency Department clinician.\nSign for the medication and complete the treatment chart.\nRing up the patient.\n\nMOQOR>\n\n|| mca3.358\n\n} A 70-year-old woman presents with a history of disturbed sleep, tearfulness, waves of\n| anxiety and chest pain for the past two weeks. Her husband had died suddenly about\nthis time a year ago of a heart attack, but in the last week she has been woken up\nby his voice calling out her name. She is alarmed and distressed by this and is worried\nshe may also be having angina or losing her mind. Her electrocardiogram (ECG)\nshows a mild sinus tachycardia only, but her troponin level is not raised and physical\nexamination is normal. Which one of the following is the most likely explanation for her\npresentation?\n\nof\n\n357"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"3.357","duplicate_group_id":"exact-d2c8f299c66c","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"bab18f6fcda6c8fc0fdb","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":375,"source_qid":"3.357"}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"tesseract","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','low','{"record_id":"bab18f6fcda6c8fc0fdb","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":375,"source_qid":"3.357","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"Ausiralian Me