| ESSCHOnS - MeniGnGONis ts 20, see i ee ee eee','{"A":"Truancy.","B":"A drop in grades at school.","C":"Angry outbursts.","D":"Arecent relationship break-up.","E":"Loss of employment. :\nMCQ 3.364\nRelatives report a gradual decline in memory and self-care skills in a 75-year-old man\nover several years. Examination reveals that he is disoriented in time, has poor short-\nterm memory, impaired planning, arithmetic and visuo spatial skills. There are no\n. other abnormal findings. Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial step\nin management?\nA. Lumbar puncture.\nB. Computed tomography (CT) of head.\nC. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain.\nD. Cholinesterase inhibitor medication.\nE. Serum electrolytes and creatinine.\nMCQ 3.365\nA previously well 40-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department complaining\n| of tightness in the chest, palpitations, blurred vision, sweating and light-headedness,\nall of which came on suddenly a few hours ago while serving on a jury. He feels\napprehensive and thinks that he has had a heart attack. He hopes that this is not so,\nas he is planning to take a holiday after the court case has finished. On examination\nhe is somewhat agitated and has cold, clammy hands. His pulse rate is 80/min and\nregular, respiratory rate is 13/min. The remainder of his physical examination is normal. ;\nElectrocardiogram (ECG) and serum cardiac enzyme troponin levels are normal, and\nyou have concluded that no further physical investigation is required. Which one of\nthe following is the most likely cause of his symptoms?\nA. Caffeine intoxication.\nB. Malingering.\nC. Conversion disorder.\nD. Panic attack.\nE. Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD).\nMCQ 3.366\nA 50-year-old woman is brought to the Emergency Department having been\nfound lying on the floor of her bedroom. She says that she had been there since\nher husband was taken to hospital 24 hours previously after collapsing with a ;\nmyocardial infarction. She had restored his pulse and breathing with cardiopulmonary\nresuscitation and this was the third time that she had done this in the past 12 |\nmonths. She had not called for help, but had been found by her daughter when\nshe had not gone to the hospital to visit her husband in the Coronary Care Unit. Her\nonly symptom is the inability to move her legs. She is able to urinate. She is not in :\nobvious distress. She cannot move her legs voluntarily, but she can sit up when\nrequested to do so. Reflexes are normal. Which one of the following is the most likely\ncause of her presentation?\n360 ,\n7"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"3.364","duplicate_group_id":"exact-3dbc18dfb705","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"9afac4432843d093c630","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":378,"source_qid":"3.364"}],"parser_version":"2.8-residual-resolver-selective-ocr-visual-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v28_selective_ocr_base_parser","text_method":"v28_selective_ocr","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,'text',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','low','{"record_id":"9afac4432843d093c630","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":378,"source_qid":"3.364","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"; Australian Medical Council as : Hs\n| ESSCHOnS - MeniGnGONis ts 20, see i ee ee eee","options":{"A":"Truancy.","B":"A drop in grades at school.","C":"Angry outbursts.","D":"Arecent relationship break-up.","E":"Loss of employment. :\nMCQ 3.364\nRelatives report a gradual decline in memory and self-care skills in a 75-year-old man\nover several years. Examination reveals that he is disoriented in time, has poor short-\nterm memory, impaired planning, arithmetic and visuo spatial skills. There are no\n. other abnormal findings. Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial step\nin management?\nA. Lumbar puncture.\nB. Computed tomography (CT) of head.\nC. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain.\nD. Cholinesterase inhibitor medication.\nE. Serum electrolytes and creatinine.\nMCQ 3.365\nA previously well 40-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department complaining\n| of tightness in the chest, palpitations, blurred vision, sweating and light-headedness,\nall of which came on suddenly a few hours ago while serving on a jury. He feels\napprehensive and thinks that he has had a heart attack. He hopes that this is not so,\nas he is planning to take a holiday after the court case has finished. On examination\nhe is somewhat agitated and has cold, clammy hands. His pulse rate is 80/min and\nregular, respiratory rate is 13/min. The remainder of his physical examination is normal. ;\nElectrocardiogram (ECG) and serum cardiac enzyme troponin levels are normal, and\nyou have concluded that no further physical investigation is required. Which one of\nthe following is the most likely cause of his symptoms?\nA. Caffeine intoxication.\nB. Malingering.\nC. Conversion disorder.\nD. Panic attack.\nE. Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD).\nMCQ 3.366\nA 50-year-old woman is brought to the Emergency Department having been\nfound lying on the floor of her bedroom. She says that she had been there since\nher husband was taken to hospital 24 hours previously after collapsing with a ;\nmyocardial infarction. She had restored his pulse and breathing with cardiopulmonary\nresuscitation and this was the third time that she had done this in the past 12 |\nmonths. She had not called for help, but had been found by her daughter when\nshe had not gone to the hospital to visit her husband in the Coronary Care Unit. Her\nonly symptom is the inability to move her legs. She is able to urinate. She is not in :\nobvious distress. She cannot move her legs voluntarily, but she can sit up when\nrequested to do so. Reflexes are normal. Which one of the following is the most likely\ncause of her presentation?\n360 ,\n7"},"source_answer":null,"source_explanation":null,"source_answer_status":"pending_import","current_answer":null,"current_answer_status":"needs_verification","current_evidence":[],"currentness_risk":"low","currentness_keywords":[],"likely_visual_question":false,"parser_confidence":"high","parser_mode":"v28_selective_ocr_base_parser","text_method":"v28_selective_ocr","record_type":"text_mcq","options_mode":"text","parser_version":"2.8-residual-resolver-selective-ocr-visual-2026-09-22","stem_hash":"3dbc18dfb7051746ca113d5cb51086558b7a3034","duplicate_group_id":"exact-3dbc18dfb705","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"9afac4432843d093c630","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":378,"source_qid":"3.364"}]}',0,'v2.9.4','2026-09-24T00:00:00.000Z') ON CONFLICT(record_id) DO UPDATE SET source_file=excluded.source_file,source_page=excluded.source_page,domain=excluded.domain,stem=excluded.stem,options_json=excluded.options_json,source_answer=excluded.source_answer,source_explanation=excluded.source_explanation,provenance_json=excluded.provenance_json,visual_asset=excluded.visual_asset,visual_url=excluded.visual_url,options_mode=excluded.options_mode,current_answer=excluded.current_answer,current_answer_status=excluded.current_answer_status,current_evidence_json=excluded.current_evidence_json,currentness_risk=excluded.currentness_risk,record_json=excluded.record_json,active_quiz=excluded.active_quiz,import_version=excluded.import_version,imported_at=excluded.imported_at;
