Section 3— Mental Health
. Malingering.
Conversion disorder.
. Panic attack.
. Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD).
Adjustment disorder.
MROEC) Ser >
MCQ 3.367
A 42-year-old accountant presents with his wife after an episode of road rage in
which she feared for her life because of his erratic and dangerous driving. She says
her husband had been stressed by recent falls in the stock market and the decreased
value of his investments. Over the past month he has had worsening insomnia, with
waves of panic as well as marked irritability and agitation. She had noticed brief
periods when his fingers would twitch and he would appear to stare vacantly for a
few seconds. On mental state examination, he is vague and confused, a poor recent
historian with impaired concentration and short-term memory. During the assessment
he suddenly becomes very angry and accuses you of having an affair with his wife.
His Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) score is 21/30. He is afebrile and his physical
examination otherwise normal. Which one of the following aspects of his history would
be most useful in clarifying the diagnosis?','{"A":"Both maternal grandparents developed dementia in their seventies.","B":"His wife has had a respiratory tract infection in the last month.","C":"A teenage son has autistic spectrum disorder.","D":"His autobiographical memory is unimpaired.","E":"He has lost his driver''s licence previously because of alcohol offences.\nMCQ 3.368\n\nA 35-year-old man who is an ex-intravenous heroin user and is now on a daily\nmethadone maintenance program, has been brought by friends to the Emergency\nDepartment after ‘collapsing’ at home within the past 30 minutes. He is usually on\n80mg methadone in daily take home dosage, but earlier that day had been given\nthree days supply as the clinic had to close for two days following a fire. His friends\nreport that he had vomited before lapsing into unconsciousness. On examination he is\ncomatose, centrally cyanosed and not responding to pain or command, his breathing\nis shallow with a respiratory rate of 8/min. His pulse rate is 56/min and thready and his\nBP is 70/SO0mmHg. After establishing an airway and 100% oxygenation, pharyngeal\nsuction and an intravenous line, which one of the following is the most important next\nstep in management?\n\n. Mannitol infusion.\nIntravenous clonidine.\n\n. Intravenous naloxone.\n\n. Sublingual buprenorphine.\nIntravenous naltrexone.\n\nMIO Cy"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"3.367","duplicate_group_id":"exact-b9d64453fb69","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"d608018e8c5315c3c4ca","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":379,"source_qid":"3.367"}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"tesseract","visual_a