MCQ 3.396
cab: A 65-year-old woman seeks advice on reducing the risk of shingles infection. Her
0g husband has ophthalmic shingles with vesicles at present and the woman wants to
ral know if she should be vaccinated. She recalls having had an attack of chicken pox
when she was 10 years old. Which one of the following is the most appropriate advice
for her regarding herpes zoster vaccination?','{"A":"She is past the age recommended for vaccination.","B":"Vaccination should be reserved for if and when she gets shingles.","C":"If she had chicken pox as a child she will not benefit from vaccination.","D":"Vaccination will reduce her risk of infection whatever her previous exposure.\naac J E. Vaccination will only reduce her risk of infection if she had not been previously\nexposed.\noe RoMeidbiotiat itt ce en cate crt ta we cera ene Th. as, (hs oh ae\n4s, MCQ 3.397","E":"A 33-year-old man presents who returned three weeks after a three week honeymoon\nes : ; edhe\n“cd in Thailand, and travelled to country areas and main cities, has presented because of\nite headaches, fever, fatigue and 2-3 loose stools daily for the last week.\n\nHe was seen by the after-hours doctor who had organised a full blood count,\nurine microscopy, urea, electrolytes, and liver function tests. Apart from a slightly\nlow platelet count (105x10%/L) and mildly raised liver enzymes (<1.5 normal) the\nremaining test results were normal. He is not sure about his vaccination status. He had\ntaken doxycycline most of the days while away, and stopped this a week ago. On\n\n| examination, he looks unwell, has a temperature of 38°C, the throat is congested and\nhe has vague epigastric tenderness. Which one of the following conditions is the most\n\nat important to exclude by a further diagnostic test?\nA. Plasmodium falciparum malaria.\nof B. Plasmodium vivax malaria.\nof C. Hepatitis A.\nD. Infectious mononucleosis.\nE. Enteric fever.\nMCQ 3.398\nA 55-year-old engineer is seen in the Emergency Department with a penetrating\nwound in the leg, sustained when he fell against machinery at work. The patient has\n_— been previously immunised against tetanus in childhood, and feels he may have\nreceived a booster dose in his twenties after a motor vehicle accident. He has no\nknown allergies. On examination he has a 3cm laceration exposing underlying fatty\nof tissue. Apart from performing wound debridement which one of the following should\ner also be given for tetanus prophylaxis?\n1e\n371"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"3.396","duplicate_group_id":"exact-3535f37613bc","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"052d7e4fa701bbd60814","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":389,"source_qid":"3.396"}],"parser_version":"2.8-residual-resolver-selective-ocr-visual-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v28_selective_ocr_base_parser","text_method":"v28_selective_ocr","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,'text',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','high','{"record_id":"052d7e4fa701bbd60814","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":389,"source_qid":"3.396","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"OES is ie Australian Medical Council\nii A. Annual mammographic screening.\n| . B. Mammographic screening each two years.\nC. Annual ultrasound.\nD. Referral to a genetic counsellor.\nE. Regular breast self-examination.\nMCQ 3.396\ncab: A 65-year-old woman seeks advice on reducing the risk of shingles infection. Her\n0g husband has ophthalmic shingles with vesicles at present and the woman wants to\nral know if she should be vaccinated. She recalls having had an attack of chicken pox\nwhen she was 10 years old. Which one of the following is the most appropriate advice\nfor her regarding herpes zoster vaccination?","options":{"A":"She is past the age recommended for vaccination.","B":"Vaccination should be reserved for if and when she gets shingles.","C":"If she had chicken pox as a child she will not benefit from vaccination.","D":"Vaccination will reduce her risk of infection whatever her previous exposure.\naac J E. Vaccination will only reduce her risk of infection if she had not been previously\nexposed.\noe RoMeidbiotiat itt ce en cate crt ta we cera ene Th. as, (hs oh ae\n4s, MCQ 3.397","E":"A 33-year-old man presents who returned three weeks after a three week honeymoon\nes : ; edhe\n“cd in Thailand, and travelled to country areas and main cities, has presented because of\nite headaches, fever, fatigue and 2-3 loose stools daily for the last week.\n\nHe was seen by the after-hours doctor who had organised a full blood count,\nurine microscopy, urea, electrolytes, and liver function tests. Apart from a slightly\nlow platelet count (105x10%/L) and mildly raised liver enzymes (<1.5 normal) the\nremaining test results were normal. He is not sure about his vaccination status. He had\ntaken doxycycline most of the days while away, and stopped this a week ago. On\n\n| examination, he looks unwell, has a temperature of 38°C, the throat is congested and\nhe has vague epigastric tenderness. Which one of the following conditions is the most\n\nat important to exclude by a further diagnostic test?\nA. Plasmodium falciparum malaria.\nof B. Plasmodium vivax malaria.\nof C. Hepatitis A.\nD. Infectious mononucleosis.\nE. Enteric fever.\nMCQ 3.398\nA 55-year-old engineer is seen in the Emergency Department with a penetrating\nwound in the leg, sustained when he fell against machinery at work. The patient has\n_— been previously immunised against tetanus in childhood, and feels he may have\nreceived a booster dose in his twenties after a motor vehicle accident. He has no\nknown allergies. On examination he has a 3cm laceration exposing underlying fatty\nof tissue. Apart from performing wound debridement which one of the following should\ner also be given for tetanus prophylaxis?\n1e\n371"},"source_answer":null,"source_explanation":null,"source_answer_status":"pending_import","curre